WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.600
Polaris missiles arrive at Cape Canaveral for their first test firings from the nuclear

00:09.600 --> 00:14.680
submarine expressly designed for the job, USS George Washington.

00:14.680 --> 00:19.860
The tests are a stirring climax to a four-year program to mate the nuclear submarine and

00:19.860 --> 00:22.340
the intermediate-range ballistic missile.

00:22.340 --> 00:25.320
Now the submissile hatches are cleared and ready.

00:25.320 --> 00:30.720
They'll house the 28-foot two-stage rockets until the moment when mighty gusts of compressed

00:30.720 --> 00:39.200
air will shoot the Polaris from under the sea to the surface and ignition.

00:39.200 --> 00:43.240
Every stage of the loading and the cruise to the firing area about 30 miles off Cape

00:43.240 --> 00:47.120
Canaveral is under detailed scrutiny of a battery of test instruments.

00:47.120 --> 00:51.920
The George Washington goes down some 50 to 60 feet below the surface and the time for

00:51.920 --> 00:55.960
launching is at hand.

00:55.960 --> 01:08.000
The first missile, after a startling off-angle emergence, corrects itself and soars down

01:08.000 --> 01:12.840
range 1,100 miles to its target with remarkable accuracy.

01:12.840 --> 01:21.560
A few hours later, the second Polaris is fired.

01:21.560 --> 01:25.880
Another successful shot, an achievement of major strategic importance for America's

01:25.880 --> 01:52.040
defense.

